.TH MAKI 8
.SH NAME
maki \- administer Maki encrypted block volumes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B maki volume
.I action config.toml
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B maki check
.I config.toml
.RB [ \-\-deep ]
.br
.B maki
.I control-command config.toml
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B maki
.I control-command
.BI \-\-socket " path"
.RI [ options ]
.br
.BR "maki \-\-help" " | " \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B volume create
initializes the backing described by the configuration.
.B volume inspect
reports the stored volume metadata and does not compare the configuration's
geometry or crypto identity with it.
Pass
.B \-\-discard
at creation to select the discard-capable format.
.B check
performs an offline check. Detach the volume before a deep check.
Both checks also compare the configuration with the volume, as attach does:
a geometry, provider, key name, compatibility id or random prefix length that
differs from what the volume stores fails the check with an
.B "ERROR: configuration mismatch:"
line that names the setting, the configured value and the volume's. The check
loads no credential and contacts no provider, so a different key under the
same name, an unavailable credential and a provider that fails its self-test
are found only at attach.
.PP
.BR "volume inspect" " and " check
first ask, on Linux, whether the user running them may write the backing
root: not on a read-only mount, a read-only bind mount, a directory without
write permission for that user or an immutable one. There they open the
backing read-only and create, write, sync and remove nothing; elsewhere, and
on other platforms, they open it as before.
A read-only check adds a
.B "warning: read-only check:"
line: what it read may exist only in the page cache, and recovery's rewrites
and repairs could not be made, so the deep check reports
.B "deep check verdict: unproven"
where a writable one says
.BR clean " or " recoverable ,
and the records recovery would replay and the journal repairs it would make
are warnings. The deep check then takes the volume lock shared, which still
excludes a daemon, and fails without running when
.I volume.lock
is missing, which it cannot create. A rollback-protected backing whose root or
witness directory cannot be written is refused: it has no read-only open.
.PP
An attached daemon exposes the
.BR status ", " metrics ", " checkpoint ", " drain " and " reload
control commands. Use a volume configuration or specify the control endpoint
with
.BI \-\-socket " path" .
A control command waits up to 60 seconds for the daemon's answer, or 600
seconds for
.BR checkpoint " and " drain ;
.BI \-\-timeout " seconds"
sets another bound. The
.BR volume " and " check
commands accept neither option.
Draining closes I/O admission and checkpoints the volume.
.PP
Run
.B maki \-\-help
for the full command list and remote-volume operations.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. A failing check, an error and a request the daemon
refuses return 1. A check that finds no error returns zero whatever its deep
verdict: zero does not mean
.BR clean .
A deep check reporting
.BR recoverable ,
or, read-only (above),
.BR unproven ,
returns zero too; a script that needs
.B clean
reads the
.B "info: deep check verdict:"
line. Invalid command usage, including a malformed option value
or approval and an option given where a configuration, a directory or a
reload section belongs, returns 2 before anything is read. Name a file
whose name starts with a dash as
.IR ./\-name .
.BR attach ", " detach " and " grow
return 3: they print the
.B maki-attach
command to run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR maki-attach (8),
.BR maki-check (8)
